Add terminal data keys for the colors a program set, with or without a change of value.

Phi needs these because it tells clients which colors a program changed with OSC 4, 10, and 11,
and a client shows its own theme for the rest. A program can set a color to its default value,
for example OSC 11 to black on a terminal whose background default is black. Comparing a current
color with its default drops that color. Ghostty already tracks the palette override mask and
the foreground and background overrides, and these keys expose them.

Upstream: https://github.com/ghostty-org/ghostty/discussions/14628

--- a/include/ghostty/vt/terminal.h
+++ b/include/ghostty/vt/terminal.h
@@ -2376,6 +2376,30 @@ typedef enum GHOSTTY_ENUM_TYPED {
    */
   GHOSTTY_TERMINAL_DATA_COLOR_PALETTE_DEFAULT = 25,
 
+  /**
+   * The palette indexes a program set with OSC 4, even when the color equals
+   * the default. OSC 104 and a full reset clear them.
+   *
+   * Output type: GhosttyColorPaletteMask *
+   */
+  GHOSTTY_TERMINAL_DATA_COLOR_PALETTE_OVERRIDES = 44,
+
+  /**
+   * Whether a program set the foreground with OSC 10, even when the color
+   * equals the default.
+   *
+   * Output type: bool *
+   */
+  GHOSTTY_TERMINAL_DATA_COLOR_FOREGROUND_OVERRIDDEN = 45,
+
+  /**
+   * Whether a program set the background with OSC 11, even when the color
+   * equals the default.
+   *
+   * Output type: bool *
+   */
+  GHOSTTY_TERMINAL_DATA_COLOR_BACKGROUND_OVERRIDDEN = 46,
+
   /**
    * The Kitty image storage limit in bytes for the active screen.
    *
